        "\n",
        "# Python Basics for `sklearn`\n",
        "\n",
        "This note introduces only the Python syntax needed for the rest of these notes. The goal is not to cover Python as a full programming language. The goal is to make code using `numpy`, `matplotlib`, and `sklearn` readable.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- The intended reader already knows R. Many ideas are the same: objects have names, functions take arguments, vectors and matrices are central, and modeling workflows usually follow a fit-then-predict pattern. -->\n",
        "\n",
        "## Running Python Code\n",
        "\n",
        "A Python code chunk looks like this:"
      ],
      "id": "461fe089"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# This is a comment\n",
        "\n",
        "x = 10      # assignment uses =\n",
        "\n",
        "a, b = 2, 3\n",
        "a + b       # last expression is automatically displayed"
      ],
      "id": "496d1715",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Notebook\n",
        "\n",
        "1. When a code cell is executed, the value of the last expression is automatically displayed. Any explicit output commands, such as `print()`, will also produce output.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. Use `#` comments only for short notes that help explain the code. Detailed explanations and discussion should be written in Markdown cells, where they are easier to read and maintain.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Indentation\n",
        "\n",
        "Python uses indentation to mark code blocks. R uses braces `{}`; Python uses indentation.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, a `for` loop:"
      ],
      "id": "ccadb320"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "for k in [1, 3, 5]:\n",
        "    print(k)"
      ],
      "id": "a095f7c2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The indented block belongs to the loop. These notes use loops only when they are helpful for plotting or comparing models.\n",
        "\n",
        "### `if` and `for`\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "The logic of `if` statements and `for` loops is straightforward. When using them, pay attention to indentation. A colon `:` indicates the beginning of an indented code block."
      ],
      "id": "87641375"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x = 1\n",
        "if x == 2:\n",
        "    print('good')\n",
        "else:\n",
        "    print('not good')"
      ],
      "id": "7beca5ec",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "for k in range(4):\n",
        "    print(k)"
      ],
      "id": "4091c756",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Functions and Objects\n",
        "\n",
        "This is not an object-oriented programming course, so don't worry too much about the details. The important idea is the distinction between a function, a class, and an object created from a class.\n",
        "\n",
        "- Function: performs an action and returns a result. Called with parentheses: `round(3.14, 2)`\n",
        "- Class: a blueprint for creating objects.\n",
        "- Object: stores information and provides methods. Accessed with a dot: `object.method()`\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Example in scikit-learn:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "model = LinearRegression()\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Here\n",
        "\n",
        "- `LinearRegression` is the class;\n",
        "- `LinearRegression()` creates a linear regression model object;\n",
        "- the object is assigned to the variable `model`.\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "coef = model.coef_\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Here\n",
        "\n",
        "- `fit()` is method;\n",
        "- `coef_` stores information about the fitted model;\n",
        "- methods are called using `object.method(...)`;\n",
        "- stored information is accessed using `object.attribute`.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "In almost all `scikit-learn` code, you should work with objects rather than classes. A common mistake is to pass a class where an object is required.\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "pipe = make_pipeline(StandardScaler, LinearRegression)     # wrong\n",
        "pipe = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), LinearRegression()) # correct\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The same idea applies to most `scikit-learn` components, including transformers, models, and pipelines. In general, if something is used as a step in a pipeline, you should create an object by adding `()`.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-caution}\n",
        "## `.` (Dot)\n",
        "\n",
        "The meaning of `.` is different in Python and R.\n",
        "\n",
        "- In Python, `.` is used to access methods and attributes of an object.\n",
        "```python\n",
        "model.fit(X, y)\n",
        "model.coef_\n",
        "```\n",
        "- In R, `.` is usually just part of a variable or function name.\n",
        "\n",
        "```r\n",
        "my.variable <- 1\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Do not interpret dots in R names as object access.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Importing Packages\n",
        "\n",
        "Python packages are loaded with `import`.\n",
        "\n",
        "The usual imports in these notes are:"
      ],
      "id": "0a2867dd"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
      ],
      "id": "e00bcfb3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The names on the right are aliases:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `numpy` is used as `np`;\n",
        "- `pandas` is used as `pd`;\n",
        "- `matplotlib.pyplot` is used as `plt`.\n",
        "\n",
        "After importing a package this way, you access its functions using the package name, e.g.\n",
        "\n",
        "- `np.array([1, 2, 3])` \n",
        "- `pd.DataFrame({\"x\": [1, 2, 3]})` \n",
        "- `plt.plot([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6])`\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "When a package is imported with `import ... as ...`, you must use the alias to access its functions and classes instead of the original name.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "Sometimes only specific functions or classes are imported."
      ],
      "id": "1fa15281"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n",
        "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split"
      ],
      "id": "afe2e07c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "These objects can then be used directly."
      ],
      "id": "0dd2fb64"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "model = LinearRegression()"
      ],
      "id": "818e5a86",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "After `from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression`, you may use `LinearRegression()` directly, but other objects from `sklearn.linear_model` are not automatically imported.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data structure\n",
        "\n",
        "### Basic Objects\n",
        "\n",
        "Python has several basic data types."
      ],
      "id": "906d41d6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x = 3          # integer\n",
        "y = 2.5        # float\n",
        "name = \"tree\"  # string\n",
        "flag = True    # Boolean"
      ],
      "id": "333e9edb",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Use `type()` to check the type of an object."
      ],
      "id": "e23de673"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "type(y)"
      ],
      "id": "3623bf79",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Python is case-sensitive. `x` and `X` are different objects.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Lists\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "A list is an **ordered collection** of objects. Lists are commonly used to store variable names, column names, or collections of parameter values.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Purpose                  | Example                                     | Explanation                                                  |\n",
        "| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n",
        "| Create a list            | `features = [\"age\", \"income\", \"education\"]` | Creates a list containing three strings.                     |\n",
        "| Access the first element | `features[0]`                               | Returns `\"age\"`. Python indexing starts at `0`.              |\n",
        "| Access the last element  | `features[-1]`                              | Returns `\"education\"`. Negative indices count from the end.  |\n",
        "| Select several elements  | `features[0:2]`                             | Returns `[\"age\", \"income\"]`. The stop index is not included. |\n",
        "| Add an element           | `features.append(\"gender\")`                 | Adds `\"gender\"` to the end of the list.                      |\n",
        "| Count elements           | `len(features)`                             | Returns the number of elements in the list.                  |\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "Unlike R, Python uses zero-based indexing, so the first element has index `0`.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Dictionaries\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "A dictionary stores **key-value pairs**. It is similar to a named list in R and is commonly used to store settings, options, and model parameters.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Purpose               | Example                                             | Explanation                                           |\n",
        "| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n",
        "| Create a dictionary   | `params = {\"n_neighbors\": 5, \"weights\": \"uniform\"}` | Creates a dictionary with two keys and their values.  |\n",
        "| Access a value        | `params[\"n_neighbors\"]`                             | Returns `5`.                                          |\n",
        "| Add or update a value | `params[\"metric\"] = \"euclidean\"`                    | Adds a new key-value pair or updates an existing one. |\n",
        "| Count entries         | `len(params)`                                       | Returns the number of key-value pairs.                |\n",
        "| Store model settings  | `{\"max_depth\": 3, \"min_samples_leaf\": 5}`           | A common use in machine learning.                     |\n",
        "| Store parameter grids | `{\"max_depth\": [2,3,4]}`                            | Often used when tuning hyperparameters.               |\n",
        "<!-- | List all keys         | `params.keys()`                                     | Returns all keys in the dictionary.                   |\n",
        "| List all values       | `params.values()`                                   | Returns all values in the dictionary.                 | -->\n",
        "\n",
        "A typical parameter grid in `scikit-learn` looks like:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "param_grid = {\n",
        "    \"max_depth\": [2, 3, 4],\n",
        "    \"min_samples_leaf\": [1, 5, 10],\n",
        "}\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "For pipelines, parameter names often use two underscores:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "param_grid = {\n",
        "    \"model__C\": [0.1, 1, 10],\n",
        "}\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "In a pipeline, a parameter name of the form `step__parameter` refers to a parameter inside a specific pipeline step. For example, `model__C` means the parameter `C` of the pipeline step named `\"model\"`. More terminologies will be discussed in related sections.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## `numpy.array`\n",
        "\n",
        "The most important data structure for numerical computation in Python is `numpy.array`."
      ],
      "id": "b413ca8b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "x = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4])\n",
        "x"
      ],
      "id": "87233c0d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Arrays have a shape and a dimension."
      ],
      "id": "12dd0524"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "print(x.shape)\n",
        "print(x.ndim)"
      ],
      "id": "7fcf0c18",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "So `x` is a 1D array containing 4 elements.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2D Arrays\n",
        "\n",
        "A 2D array is similar to a matrix."
      ],
      "id": "f17cc595"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X = np.array([\n",
        "    [1, 2],\n",
        "    [3, 4],\n",
        "    [5, 6]\n",
        "])\n",
        "\n",
        "X"
      ],
      "id": "ef813068",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "print(X.shape)\n",
        "print(X.ndim)"
      ],
      "id": "ab1e1e54",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Here `X` is a 2D array with 3 rows and 2 columns.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Indexing\n",
        "\n",
        "Use `[row, column]` indexing."
      ],
      "id": "56e0f51a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "X[0, 1]      # row 0, column 1\n",
        "X[:, 0]      # first column\n",
        "X[1, :]      # second row"
      ],
      "id": "a098e9a1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The symbol `:` means \"all indices\" along that axis.\n",
        "\n",
        "Slicing can select ranges."
      ],
      "id": "a261763e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "X[0:2, 1]      # first 2 rows, column 1\n",
        "X[0, 0:2]      # row 0, first 2 columns\n",
        "X[0:2, 0:2]    # upper-left 2x2 block"
      ],
      "id": "e48e38fa",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "`X[:, 0]` returns a 1D array with shape `(n,)`, not a column vector.\n",
        "\n",
        "To obtain a column vector, use"
      ],
      "id": "ac48e88c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "X[:, [0]]\n",
        "# or\n",
        "X[:, 0].reshape(-1, 1)"
      ],
      "id": "50bd156f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "which both have shape `(n, 1)`.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "### Common Operations\n",
        "\n",
        "Arrays support vectorized arithmetic."
      ],
      "id": "d8e79327"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "x + 1\n",
        "x * 2\n",
        "np.mean(x)"
      ],
      "id": "1755846d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Operations are applied elementwise.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Axis-wise \n",
        "\n",
        "Many functions accept an `axis=` argument. "
      ],
      "id": "b96728b2"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X              # show X"
      ],
      "id": "8b099867",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Computes the sum across rows: "
      ],
      "id": "88de8690"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X.sum(axis=0)"
      ],
      "id": "b61d107f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Computes the mean across columns:"
      ],
      "id": "257d7161"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X.mean(axis=1)"
      ],
      "id": "06530953",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "A useful rule:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `axis=0`: operate down rows (one result per column)\n",
        "- `axis=1`: operate across columns (one result per row)\n",
        "\n",
        "### Reshaping\n",
        "\n",
        "A 1D array can be converted into a matrix. Consider a 1D array"
      ],
      "id": "c5fdf74f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4])"
      ],
      "id": "4a1ffd61",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "To create a column vector:"
      ],
      "id": "e0a5f796"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x.reshape(-1, 1)"
      ],
      "id": "45a9bc4c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "To create a row vector:"
      ],
      "id": "8289bcbe"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x.reshape(1, -1)"
      ],
      "id": "12d1e8cf",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The value `-1` means \"infer this dimension automatically.\"\n",
        "\n",
        "A common pattern is"
      ],
      "id": "ef4a25ad"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "test_x = np.linspace(0, 1, 101).reshape(-1, 1)"
      ],
      "id": "c5d6a092",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "On the other side, to flatten an array back to 1D:"
      ],
      "id": "826205b5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X.reshape(-1)"
      ],
      "id": "e5aacc25",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "`NumPy` reads entries row by row and produces a one-dimensional array.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "Other flattening methods include `.ravel()` and `.flatten()`. The difference is about how copy-and-view is handled. We mainly use `.reshape(-1)` because it makes the resulting shape explicit.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "One common source of bugs in statistical learning is using arrays with incorrect shapes.\n",
        "\n",
        "In `scikit-learn`, predictors `X` are usually stored as a 2D array and responses `y` as a 1D array. Other libraries may use different conventions, and the required shapes often depend on the specific model or loss function.\n",
        "\n",
        "When shape-related errors occur, always check the documentation of the library you are using.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "### Arrays versus Lists\n",
        "\n",
        "Lists are general-purpose containers, and its addition concatenates. For example"
      ],
      "id": "e5f4acf7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "[1, 2, 3] + [4, 5, 6]"
      ],
      "id": "f842af61",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Arrays are designed for numerical computation. Its operations are align with the regular math operations. Therefore for numerical work and machine learning, use `numpy.array` whenever possible.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Random Numbers\n",
        "\n",
        "These notes often use `numpy` random number generators for simulation."
      ],
      "id": "155a0f0f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "rng = np.random.default_rng(1)\n",
        "\n",
        "x = rng.normal(size=5)\n",
        "x"
      ],
      "id": "4565e4c7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "\n",
        "The number `1` is a seed. It makes the random output reproducible.\n",
        "\n",
        "On a single machine, if the seed is fixed the results are supposed to be the same.\n",
        "\n",
        "You may choose any number as the seed. You may use the same seed when you want to see the same result.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Some common random functions are:"
      ],
      "id": "b6b6ad9b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
