    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## `pandas`\n",
        "\n",
        "`pandas` can be treated as a wrapper of `numpy.array` which make it more readable. The complete `pandas` is very complicated. Here we only mention a few very important concepts.\n",
        "\n",
        "To import `pandas`, in most cases we set `pd` as its alias."
      ],
      "id": "b4095f49"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd"
      ],
      "id": "fa876d54",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### `DataFrame` and `Series`\n",
        "\n",
        "In `pandas`, `DataFrame` is the 2D data table, and `Series` is the 1D column. There are many ways to create a `DataFrame`. Here we focus on one of them, that is to convert a `dict` into a `DataFrame`."
      ],
      "id": "2d014b56"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "results = {\n",
        "    'round': [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],\n",
        "    'score': [0.22, 0.33, 0.44, 0.55, 0.66], \n",
        "    'mse': [0.12, 0.11, 0.10, 0.09, 0.08]\n",
        "}\n",
        "df_res = pd.DataFrame(results)\n",
        "df_res"
      ],
      "id": "28db2e5d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "A single column is a `Series`."
      ],
      "id": "e6e4645b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df_res['score']"
      ],
      "id": "bf9641e3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Note that similar to the `numpy.array` case, if we select the column as a set (even if the set contains only 1 element), we will get a 2D object, which is a `DataFrame` in this case."
      ],
      "id": "991dbcb8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df_res[['score']]"
      ],
      "id": "6c3ce1e2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Read and write files\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "In `scikit-learn`, predictors and responses can be stored either as `numpy` arrays or as `pandas` objects. Throughout this course, many examples use `numpy`, but most code works equally well with `pandas` data structures.\n",
        "\n",
        "When working with external datasets, a common workflow is:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Read the data into a `DataFrame`.\n",
        "2. Perform data cleaning and preprocessing.\n",
        "3. Extract predictors and responses.\n",
        "4. Fit a statistical learning model.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Two of the most common tabular file formats are CSV files and Excel spreadsheets.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n",
        "## CSV\n",
        "\n",
        "CSV stands for comma-separated values. A CSV file is a plain-text file that stores tabular data using a separator between columns.\n",
        "\n",
        "Common separators include:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `,` (comma)\n",
        "- `;` (semicolon)\n",
        "- `\\t` (tab)\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the following file [`example.csv`](example.csv).\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "round\tscore\tmse\n",
        "1\t0.220000\t0.120000\n",
        "2\t0.330000\t0.110000\n",
        "3\t0.440000\t0.100000\n",
        "4\t0.550000\t0.090000\n",
        "5\t0.660000\t0.080000\n",
        "```\n",
        "In Python we could use `pd.read_csv()` to read it.\n"
      ],
      "id": "7889048e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "df = pd.read_csv('example.csv')\n",
        "df"
      ],
      "id": "3f773e86",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The result is incorrect because this file uses tab characters (`\\t`) rather than commas as separators.\n",
        "\n",
        "Specify the separator explicitly:"
      ],
      "id": "6162b37e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df = pd.read_csv('example.csv', sep='\\t')\n",
        "df"
      ],
      "id": "44a34873",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n",
        "## Excel\n",
        "Excel spreadsheets can be read using `pd.read_excel()`. We provide the [`exmaple.xlsx`](example.xlsx) as an example.\n"
      ],
      "id": "b910dc30"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "df = pd.read_excel('example.xlsx')\n",
        "df"
      ],
      "id": "c864c161",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Unlike CSV files, Excel files are stored in a binary format. Therefore, `pandas` requires an additional package called an engine to read and write them. The most commonly used engine is `openpyxl`.\n",
        "\n",
        "```bash\n",
        "uv add openpyxl\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "In this course, `openpyxl` is already included in the project's `pyproject.toml`, so no additional installation is required.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Writing files is similar to reading files. The main difference is that writing starts from an existing `DataFrame` instead of starting from `pd`.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "df.to_csv('example2.csv')\n",
        "df.to_excel('example2.xlsx')\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Relative paths\n",
        "\n",
        "In this course, always use relative paths when reading or writing files. \n",
        "\n",
        "A relative path starts from the current working directory rather than from the root of the file system.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose your working directory is\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "C:/Users/WDAGUtilityAccount/Project/\n",
        "```\n",
        "and you have the following structure:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "Project/\n",
        "├── example.csv\n",
        "└── foldername/\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Then you can read and write files using:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "df = pd.read_csv(\"example.csv\")\n",
        "df.to_csv(\"foldername/result.csv\")\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Notice that neither path begins with a drive name such as `C:` or `D:`.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "If your files are stored outside the working directory, **copy them into the working directory** before reading them.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "Although absolute paths such as\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "pd.read_csv(\"D:/Files/example.csv\")\n",
        "```\n",
        "often work on your own computer, they may fail on another computer because the file locations are different.\n",
        "\n",
        "Using relative paths makes your code more portable and easier to share.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## `matplotlib` Basics\n",
        "\n",
        "These notes use only basic `matplotlib`. The standard import is:"
      ],
      "id": "e5d4751e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
      ],
      "id": "3e15d50c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We provide some code examples here. Most of the commands are straightforward. Please check the [official document](https://matplotlib.org/) if you need more details.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Scatter Plot\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- fmt: off -->"
      ],
      "id": "ecd476b0"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4])\n",
        "y = np.array([1.1, 2.0, 2.8, 4.2])\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.scatter(x, y)\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"x\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"y\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Scatter Plot\");"
      ],
      "id": "d53b967c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Line Plot"
      ],
      "id": "f9781d13"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x_grid = np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 200)\n",
        "y_grid = np.sin(x_grid)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.plot(x_grid, y_grid)\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"x\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"sin(x)\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Line Plot\");"
      ],
      "id": "db28202a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Scatter and Line Together"
      ],
      "id": "9781f710"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "rng = np.random.default_rng(1)\n",
        "\n",
        "x = rng.uniform(0, 2 * np.pi, size=30)\n",
        "y = np.sin(x) + rng.normal(scale=0.2, size=30)\n",
        "x_grid = np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 200)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.scatter(x, y, facecolors=\"none\", edgecolors=\"black\")\n",
        "plt.plot(x_grid, np.sin(x_grid), color=\"C1\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"x\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"y\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Data and Curve\");"
      ],
      "id": "06c74d82",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This pattern appears often when we plot data points and a fitted curve.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Multiple Plots\n",
        "\n",
        "Use `plt.subplots` for multiple panels."
      ],
      "id": "f9884297"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(10, 4))\n",
        "\n",
        "axs[0].scatter(x, y)\n",
        "axs[0].set_title(\"scatter\")\n",
        "\n",
        "axs[1].plot(x_grid, np.sin(x_grid))\n",
        "axs[1].set_title(\"line\");"
      ],
      "id": "0ef7dcdc",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "\n",
        "Notice that when using an axes object, many plotting commands have different names. For example, `plt.title()` becomes `ax.set_title()`. Therefore, you cannot always replace `plt` with `ax` directly. For details, consult the official documentation.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "### Histograms"
